Templates form Moth's string-building engine.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    name = "Priya"

    greeting = [:
        Hello, [name]
    ]
]

Square brackets create the template.

The colon separates its head from its body.

A nested template inserts a value from the surrounding scope.

Templates can build plain text, Markdown, HTML, CSS or another string-based
format. They may be finished during compilation or built at runtime.

HTML pages come from `@entry.moth`. A direct top-level template in that file
becomes page output. Builder metadata uses direct const root `$in` inserts. No
root-purpose directive exists, and the former `$page` metadata design is
superseded. See @../progress (Progress) for current support and
@../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots) for page
metadata. Assigned or returned templates remain ordinary `String` values.
